  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/bms#mainPipeUnitWeight">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="http://example.org/bms#GasSupplySystem"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">main pipe unit weight</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Unit weight of the steel gas main pipe, w_main (kg/m). Default DN40 ≈ 4.05 kg/m per ASME B36.10 Schedule 40 \citep{engtoolboxSteelPipe}. Replaces the previous "mainPipeDiameterFactor"; see riserPipeUnitWeight comment for rationale.</rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:nodeID="Nb67dcfaa8c2745bb9950d7464a36417a">
    <rdf:first rdf:resource="http://example.org/bms#PlumbingSystem"/>
    <rdf:rest rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:nodeID="Nc36ce9df17e84813a309cb884f0f691a">
    <rdf:first rdf:resource="http://example.org/bms#Region"/>
    <rdf:rest rdf:nodeID="N2d5cf2116b954a34ab494e441a67375a"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/bms#PlumbingSystem">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://example.org/bms#BuildingSystem"/>
    <calculationFormula>Water supply pipe: L_pipe=alpha_waterpipe*GFA (alpha≈0.2 m/m2); S=L_pipe*w. Sanitary fixtures: N_unit=GFA/A_unit; N_fixture=N_unit*alpha_fixture (alpha≈1-2/unit); S=N_fixture*w*p_i. Drainage pipe: L_drain=GFA*alpha_drain (main: 1.5–2 m/m2; branch: 0.08 m/m2); S=L_drain*w. Water tank: eps=n*Q [L/day, daily demand]; V_tank=eps*f [L; tank sized for ~1-day peak storage — f is a peak-demand factor, NOT a unit conversion]; A_surface=6*V_tank^(2/3) [cube tank]; S_steel=A_surface*t_steel*rho_steel. Water heater: N_unit=GFA/A_unit; N_heater=N_unit*alpha_heater; S=N_heater*w*p_i. Pump: P=lambda1*V_tank*(H+H_friction) [V_tank reused from the Water Tank row, i.e. pump sized to lift one tank's worth of water; lambda1 is an empirical constant absorbing unit conversion and assumed efficiency, NOT derived from the standard P=rho*g*Q*H/eta formula with an explicit flow rate Q]; S=P*w.</calculationFormula>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">DATA PROVENANCE NOTE: alpha_waterpipe (water supply pipe density), alpha_fixture (sanitary fixture density), alpha_drain (main/branch drainage pipe density), and lambda1 (pump power coefficient) are preliminary engineering design heuristics, not values codified in a single published standard. Relevant Singapore standards exist — SS636:2018 Code of Practice for Water Services (formerly CP48:2005) for water supply, and the PUB Code of Practice on Sewerage and Sanitary Works (3rd Edition, 2025) for fixtures/drainage — but their specific numeric design tables could not be independently verified at the time of writing (source documents were access-protected). Treat these factors as adjustable defaults pending verification against the full code text.</rdfs:comment>
  </rdf:Description>
